===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pel- (beat)}}
From {{inh|en|enm|felt}}, from {{inh|en|ang|felt}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*felt}} (compare {{cog|nl|vilt}}, {{cog|de|Filz}}, {{cog|da|filt}}, {{cog|fr|feutre}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*pilto}}, {{m|ine-pro|*pilso}} 'felt' (compare {{cog|la|pilleus||felt|pos=adjective}}, {{cog|cu|плъсть}}, {{cog|sq|plis}}, Ancient Greek {{m|grc|πῖλος}}), from *''pel-'' 'to beat'. More at [[anvil]].

====Noun====
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[cloth]] or stuff made of [[mat]]ted [[fibre]]s of [[wool]], or wool and [[fur]], [[full]]ed or wrought into a compact substance by rolling and pressure, with lees or size, without spinning or weaving.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|IV|6|passage=It were a delicate stratagem to shoe <br> A troop of horse with '''felt'''.}}
# A [[hat]] made of felt.
# A [[felt-tip pen]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1989|author=Anne D. Forester; Margaret Reinhard|title=The Learners' Way|page=116
|passage=You'll notice that all the illustrations are done in different media: some with pencil crayons, some with '''felts''', some with paint, some with chalk pastels.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[skin]] or [[hide]]; a [[fell]]; a [[pelt]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1707|author=John Mortimer|title=The whole art of husbandry|passage=To know whether sheep are sound or not, see that the '''felt''' be loose.}}

